INVWIN.HTX - help texts of RETROINV for Windows (INVWIN).

Each section starts with a line "@TOPIC" and ends at the next such
line; its first line is its title.  Lines before the first section,
like these, are not shown.  Lines hold at most WH_HELPW characters
(WHELP.H), the width of the help window.

The build (TEST/hlpchk.py) writes the help window's file, an index of
the sections before these lines, through which the program reads a
section with one read, and the Windows Help source, which HC.EXE
compiles into INVWIN.HLP.  There a paragraph is wrapped; a list of
keys, lines that start with a blank, each a key, two blanks or more
and its description, continued by lines indented deeper, becomes an
entry per key, its description wrapped beside it; another paragraph
with a line that starts with a blank keeps its lines and spaces; a
numbered list wraps each "N. " item; a paragraph "See also: " lists
titles of other sections, which become jumps.
Section INDEX is Windows Help's alone: its title is the help file's,
and a jump to every other section but the fields' follows its text.
A section TOPIC_FIELD holds the help of field FIELD of the form of
topic TOPIC, which F1 in that field shows; the form's topic ends with
a jump to each of its fields' sections, and each jumps back.
@INDEX
RETROINV for Windows

RETROINV keeps a list of your equipment, where it is kept, what was
done to it and who borrowed it.  Choose a topic, or press F1 in the
program for the help of the list or dialog in use; in a form, that
of the field with the focus.

@LOGIN
Logging in

Type your user name, Tab, then your password, and press Enter.  The
password is not shown while you type it.  User names are upper case;
the password is checked exactly as typed.

After three failed attempts the session ends and the program stops.
An account that must change its password goes to the password dialog
first.

A new, empty data directory has one user, ADMIN.  The sample data has
three: ADMIN (level 3), OPER (level 2) and READER (level 1).  The
first password is the user name in lower case (admin, oper, reader).
Each must choose a new password at its first login.

Esc, or Cancel, leaves the program.
@START
Getting started

RETROINV keeps a list of your equipment: what each item is, where it
is kept, what was done to it, who borrowed it and what it is reserved
for or used with.

1. Make the places first.  Locations > New location adds a room; in
   Locations > Browse locations, Ins on a room adds a shelf, a box or
   a bin inside it, down to eight levels.
2. Then add the items.  Items > New item, or Ins in an item list,
   opens a new item: give it a category and choose its location with
   F4.  Enter on a location lists the items kept there, and Ins in
   that list adds one already placed in it.
3. An assembly is an item built of others: a PC with its cards and
   drives.  Select a card and choose Record > Install in to install
   it in the PC, or open the PC's parts (Record > Parts) and press
   Ins.  Parts go wherever their assembly goes; Items > Assemblies
   lists the assemblies.

Ctrl+L on an item lends it, Ctrl+R reserves it and Ctrl+U marks it in
use with another item, such as a monitor with a PC; Items > Loans and
holds lists them all.  Record > Service records on an item lists its
service records, such as repairs and cleaning.  Users of level 1 may
only look, level 2 may change the inventory, and level 3 also manages
the users and the data files.

F1 shows the help of the window in front, and Help > Keyboard the keys
that work everywhere.

See also: Item lists; Locations; Keys everywhere.
@PASSWD
Changing the password

Type the old password, then the new one twice, and press Enter.  A
password is made of printable characters, without spaces, and must
differ from the old one; one too short or too long is refused, and
the message gives the lengths allowed.  None of them is shown while
you type.

  Tab, Shift+Tab     next, previous field
  Enter              change             Esc             cancel

When the change is required after logging in, the Cancel button reads
Log out and logs out.
@ITEMS
Item lists

  Up, Down, PgUp, PgDn, Home, End   move through the list
  Left, Right  scroll the columns sideways, as the bar at the foot
               of a list wider than its window does
  Shift+Up, Shift+Down   select the rows passed over too
  Shift+click  select the rows up to the one clicked
  Ctrl+click   add the row clicked to the selection, or take it away
  Enter        open the selected item (a double click too)
  Ctrl+E       edit the selected item (level 2)
  Ins          a new item (level 2)
  Del          delete the selected item, after asking (level 2)
  Ctrl+D       duplicate the selected item and edit the copy; Esc
               there, with nothing saved, removes the copy again
               (level 2)
  Ctrl+L       lend the selected item (level 2)
  Ctrl+R       reserve it for someone or for another item (level 2)
  Ctrl+U       mark it in use with another item or a place (level 2)
  Shift+F8     add mode (below)
  Ctrl+/       select every row of the list, on every page (below)
  Ctrl+\       select the row with the dotted frame alone
  F6, Tab      to the preview beside the list, and back (Shift+F6
               and Shift+Tab too); Esc there comes back as well
  Shift+F10    the Record menu at the selected row

Beside the list, on the right, a preview shows the selected item: its
ID, maker and model, what the item form's Overview page says of it,
with the place where it is kept, and its notes.  It follows the
selection once it rests on an item for a moment.  With the focus in
it Up, Down, PgUp, PgDn, Home and End scroll it, Enter opens the
item, F5 reads it again, and the keys and the Record menu act on that
item as they do in the list; a click with the right mouse button on
it, or Shift+F10, opens that menu there.  Drag the bar between the
two with the mouse to widen either.  View > Pane beside the list
hides the preview, or shows it again; both are kept in the settings
file (WINPANE.ITEM=).

On a serial line or a mailbox the list is shown alone until View >
Pane beside the list asks for the preview, as reading each item
holds the program for a while.  There the preview waits longer before
it follows, still showing the item before, and says "Loading item"
with the item's ID while it reads.  Esc while it reads stops it: the
preview then says "Not loaded: press Enter here", and Enter in it
reads the item after all.

The Record menu (Alt+C) also has the commands without a key, at
level 2: Move, the selected item with its parts to a location (type
its code, or choose it with F4); Install in, the item in another
(type its ID, or find it with F4); Take out, the item out of its
assembly to a location; and Split units, units of the item into a new
item (type how many).  At every level it lists the selected item's
Parts, Service records, Loans and holds, and what is Used with it, and
Next order sorts the full list by ID, location, maker or serial.

The title bar names the list and its order, and its heading shows an
arrow; a click on the ID, Maker, Location or Serial number heading
lists the items in that order, as Next order does.  The Category
column shows each category's code and name; Items > Browse by
category lists the items category by category (see Items by
category).  Items > Assemblies lists
the assemblies: the items built of others that are installed in none.
Beside them, in place of the preview, stand the parts of the selected
assembly, with the keys and commands of the parts' own list (see
Parts of an item): F6 or Tab goes there and back, Ins installs a
part in that assembly, and Take out or Move on either side shows
both again as they then are.  Once the last part of the selected
assembly is taken out it is no assembly: it leaves the list, which
takes the keys again.  Enter on an assembly still opens the item.
View > Pane beside the list hides the parts or shows them again,
kept in the settings file apart from the preview (WINPANE.ASSY=).
Works says whether the item works: yes, no, intrm (intermittently) or
? (not tested).  The Location column names the place where each item
is; it is empty for an item whose location is not known.  The last
column says why an item cannot be lent or held now: all its units on
loan, reserved or in use, installed in another item, disposed of, or
none left.  On the results of a search Esc goes back to the search
with its values kept.  In the dialogs of Move and Take out the line
under the location shows where it is; (no location), the first line of
the list F4 shows, is for an item whose place is not known.  In the
dialog of Install in, the item is given by its ID, or by text: F4 or
the ... button lists the items holding it to choose from, or every
item when the field is empty, and Enter on text does the same.  Digits
that are no item's ID are looked for as text, as models and serial
numbers can be all digits (5150, 0012345).

A split leaves the item with fewer units, keeping its serial number,
its parts, loans and holds, and makes a new item of the units split
off, a copy of it lying loose at its location; it must keep at least
one unit, and every unit on loan, reserved or in use.

Several rows can be selected, among the rows shown: the status bar
then says how many ("7 selected"), and a move to another page, a new
order or Home drops them; Shift with an arrow key at the top or bottom
of the rows shown beeps and keeps them instead.  Del, Move, Install
in, Take out, Ctrl+L, Ctrl+R and Ctrl+U then act on every selected
row: they ask once, for the first, and go on through the others, the
status bar counting them; the first refusal stops them, with a box
saying how many were done.  Enter, Ctrl+E and the other commands act
on the row with the dotted frame alone.  The
parts, a location's contents and the loans and holds select several
rows the same way.

Shift+F8 turns on add mode, as in File Manager, for rows far apart:
the arrow keys then move the dotted frame and leave the selection as
it is, Space selects or clears the row in the frame, and Shift with an
arrow key or a click adds the rows up to it.  Shift+F8 again, or a
plain click, ends it; meanwhile the status bar says "Shift+F8 add
mode".  The preview shows the one selected item, wherever the dotted
frame is, and with several selected the item in the frame.

Ctrl+/, or Record > Select all, selects every row of the list, not
only the rows shown, as in File Manager: the status bar says "All 260
selected", and PgUp, PgDn, Home, End and the scroll bar look through
them.  Del, Move, Install in, Take out, Ctrl+L, Ctrl+R and Ctrl+U
then act on every one, as on several rows, Del asking first with
their number.  An arrow key, a click, another order, or a command
that shows the list again leaves the row with the dotted frame alone
selected, as Ctrl+\ does at any time.  The rows are counted first,
which Esc stops.  A list of more than 4096 rows is too long to select
at once: narrow it first, with Find or a filter.  A Select all that
Esc stops, or that the list is too long for, leaves selected what was
selected before.  As in File Manager, Ctrl+/ and Ctrl+\ are the keys
where / and \ sit on a US keyboard; on another keyboard use Record >
Select all.

See also: The item form; Parts of an item; Search; Items by category.
@ITEM
The item form

  Tab, Shift+Tab     next, previous field
  Ctrl+PgDn          next page: Overview, Technical, Acquisition,
                     Notes; Ctrl+PgUp the previous one
  PgUp, PgDn         first, last field of the page
  Enter              save               Esc             cancel
  F4, Alt+Down       open the list of the category, status, condition
                     or works, choose the location, or add a tag
  Ctrl+Enter         a new line in the notes

The ID, maker and model stand above the pages, which a click on a tab,
or Alt and its underlined letter, shows too; Alt and a field's letter
shows the field's page.

Category, Status, Condition and Works are codes, chosen from the list
of the field, which shows each code with its name: F4 or Alt+Down
opens it, and a letter typed selects the next code starting with it;
Enter closes the open list, Esc too, back on the code it had.
Dates are typed as YYYY-MM-DD and numbers without separators; shown in
the lists and in fields that cannot be changed, they take the formats
of Control Panel's International settings.  Opened with Enter the form
only shows the item, its values as text, a long one in a box that
scrolls; Tab moves among the tabs, those boxes and the buttons, and
Enter, Esc or Close closes it; its Edit
button or Ctrl+E, there or in the item list, opens it for editing
(level 2).  The buttons Parts, Service, Loans and Used with show the
item's parts, service records, loans and holds, and what is used with
it, Esc there coming back to the form; they work while the form only
shows the item, or while editing as long as nothing has been changed;
save with Enter first otherwise.  Leaving a changed form asks first.

See also: Parts of an item; Service records; Loans and holds.
@ITEM_ITEM_ID
Item: Item ID

The item's number, up to 8 digits, which the program gives a new item
when it is saved.  It cannot be changed; labels, reports and the item
fields of other forms name the item by it.
@ITEM_CATEGORY
Item: Category

The item's category, a code of up to 4 letters and digits, chosen
from the list of the categories, which shows each code with its name.
Required.
@ITEM_STATUS
Item: Status

A active, D disposed of.  An item with units on loan, reserved or in
use cannot be disposed of or deleted, nor can one that others are
reserved for or used with: release them first.
@ITEM_ITEMTYPE
Item: Type

What kind of thing the item is, in words of your own, such as
Computer, Monitor or Disk drive: up to 20 characters.
@ITEM_COND
Item: Condition

The looks alone: NEW new, EXC excellent, GD good, FR fair, PR poor,
or empty, not graded.  Whether it works is the Works field's.
@ITEM_MFR
Item: Maker

The maker's name, such as IBM or Commodore: up to 30 characters.  The
item lists can be sorted by it.
@ITEM_MODEL
Item: Model

The model's name or number, such as 5150 or PET 2001: up to 30
characters.
@ITEM_SERIALNO
Item: Serial

The serial number as it is written on the item: up to 30 characters.
The item lists can be sorted by it, and Search finds an item by it.
@ITEM_DESCR
Item: Description

A line about the item, such as its fittings or its origin: up to 60
characters.
@ITEM_WORKS
Item: Works

? not tested (a new item's and a copy's), Y yes, I works
intermittently, N no; only you set it.  Note a test in the item's
service records.
@ITEM_QTY
Item: Quantity

How many units the item stands for, such as a box of 25 disks: up to
5 digits, 1 for a single thing.  It cannot go below the units on loan,
reserved or in use.
@ITEM_HELD
Item: Units held

The line beside Quantity says how many units are on loan, reserved or
in use, or how many are available, and how many things are used with
the item.  It cannot be changed here: the Loans button lists the loans
and holds, Used with what is used with the item.
@ITEM_LOC_ID
Item: Location

Where the item is kept: choose it from the list of locations that F4
in the field, or a click on the ... button beside it, shows, or type
its code, up to 10 characters.  Leave it empty when the place is not
known.  In that list Enter, OK or a double click takes the line
chosen, and a letter moves to the next location whose name starts
with it; Esc or Cancel leaves the field as it was.  A part lies at the
location of the item it is installed in.
@ITEM_PARENT_ID
Item: Part of

The ID, maker and model of the item this one is installed in, empty
when it stands alone.  It cannot be changed here: install and take out
parts in the list of the parts (the Parts button).
@ITEM_WHERE
Item: Where

The line under Location shows where the location is, such as Office >
Shelf A > Bin 12, also for a location without a code, which the field
shows by its number.
@ITEM_ACQ_DATE
Item: Date acquired

The day the item was bought or given, typed as YYYY-MM-DD; empty when
not known.
@ITEM_ACQ_SRC
Item: Acquired from

Where the item came from, such as a shop, an auction or a person: up
to 30 characters.
@ITEM_ACQ_PRICE
Item: Price paid

What the item cost, a number with at most two decimals, typed without
separators; empty when not known.  The valuation report totals it.
@ITEM_CUR_VALUE
Item: Current value

What the item is worth today, a number with at most two decimals,
typed without separators; empty when not known.  The valuation report
totals it.
@ITEM_TAGS
Item: Tags

Words of your own, such as retro or a project, between commas or
spaces, up to 60 characters in all; each tag 1 to 12 of A-Z 0-9 - _ .
/ +.  F4 in Tags, or its ... button, lists the tags in use (at most
50, A to Z; Esc while they load lists those found so far), its title
saying when that is not all of them: Enter, OK or a double click adds
the one chosen after a comma, unless the field holds it already; one
that does not fit is refused under the fields.  A save shows the tags
as kept: one comma between them, a repeat left out.
@ITEM_CREATED
Item: Created

The day the item was first saved, set by the program.
@ITEM_UPDATED
Item: Updated

The day the item was last saved, set by the program.
@ITEM_NOTES
Item: Notes

Free text about the item, on the Notes page, Ctrl+Enter starting a
new line.  Notes too long to be edited here are shown read-only, as
the message under the fields says.
@PARTS
Parts of an item

  Up, Down, PgUp, PgDn, Home, End   move through the list
  Left, Right  scroll the columns sideways, as the bar at the foot
               of a list wider than its window does
  Shift+Up, Shift+Down   select the rows passed over too
  Shift+click  select the rows up to the one clicked
  Ctrl+click   add the row clicked to the selection, or take it away
  Enter        open the selected part in the item form
  Ctrl+E       edit the selected part (level 2)
  Ins          install an item in this one: type its ID, or find it
               with F4 (level 2)
  Esc          back to the list or the item form it was opened from;
               beside the assemblies, back to them
  Shift+F10    the Record menu at the selected row

Ins is Record > Install part, and Record > Take out takes the selected
part out of its assembly, to a location: type its code, or choose it
with F4 (level 2).

The list shows every part below the item, each part followed by its
own parts, indented one step per level.  A part keeps its own parts
when it is installed or taken out, and an installed part always lies
at the location of the item it is built into.  An item on loan,
reserved or in use cannot be installed, nor one that would end up
holding itself, nor one that would make the assembly too deep or too
large; the message says which.
@LOCS
Locations

Every place things are kept, as a tree: rooms, and inside them
shelves, racks, boxes or bins, down to eight levels.  Each location is
listed under the one it is inside, indented, with its type, its code
and its capacity.  Items can be kept at any level.

The tree is on the left, and on the right the items at the selected
location, which follow the selection once it rests on a location for a
moment.  Drag the bar between them with the mouse to widen either; the
width is kept in the settings file (WINSPLIT=).  The Record menu, the
tool bar and the count on the status bar are those of the side that
has the focus.  Drag an item from the right onto a location in the
tree to move it there, as Record > Move does (level 2), or several
selected items, from one of them; Esc, or letting go anywhere else,
leaves them where they are.

On a serial line or a mailbox the tree is shown alone, as fetching
the items would hold the program at each location: Enter opens them.
View > Pane beside the list says otherwise, there and on any other
connection, and the settings file keeps it: WINPANE.LOC=1 shows the
items beside the tree, WINPANE.LOC=0 the tree alone.

  Up, Down, PgUp, PgDn, Home, End   move through the list
  Left, Right  scroll the columns sideways, as the bar at the foot
               of a list wider than its window does
  letter       the next location whose name starts with it
  F6, Tab      to the items beside the tree, and back (Shift+F6 and
               Shift+Tab too); Esc there comes back as well
  Enter        the items at the selected location as a list of
               their own (a double click too)
  Ctrl+E       edit the selected location (level 2)
  Ins          a new location inside the selected one (level 2); in
               an empty list, at the top level
  Del          delete the selected location, after asking (level 2);
               only a location without items and without locations
               inside it can be deleted
  Esc          back to the item list
  Shift+F10    the Record menu at the selected row

Record > Move moves the selected location, with everything inside it,
into another chosen from the list, or to the top level (level 2).  A
location cannot go inside itself or one inside it, nor inside one at
the eighth level: the list, the form's Inside or Ins says so at once.
A location list opens on the current location, but in a very long
tree its search may stop short: OK on the row where it stopped then
asks before choosing it.
Locations > New location, or a new location whose Inside is left
empty, adds one at the top level.  To find the items whose location is
not known, use Search with Location set to "(no location)".
Locations > Location types lists the types a location can have.

See also: The location form; Contents of a location.
@LOC
The location form

  Tab, Shift+Tab     next, previous field
  PgUp, PgDn         first, last field
  Enter              save               Esc             cancel
  F4, Alt+Down       open the list of the types, or choose Inside
  Ctrl+PgDn          the notes' page, and Ctrl+PgUp back
  Ctrl+Enter         a new line in the notes

A location is a place where items are kept, such as a room, a shelf
or a box, and may lie inside another.  Name and Type are required.
@LOC_LOC_NAME
Location: Name

What the location is called, such as Office or Shelf A: up to 30
characters.  Two locations inside the same one cannot have the same
name.
@LOC_LTYPE
Location: Type

A code of up to 8 letters and digits, such as ROOM, SHELF or BOX,
chosen from the list of the types with their names; a new location
starts with the type of the others in the same place.  Required.
@LOC_PARENT_ID
Location: Inside

The location it is in, chosen with F4 or typed as its code, up to 10
characters; empty for the top level.  Changing it moves the location
with everything inside it; a location cannot go inside itself, nor
inside one at the deepest level.
@LOC_WHERE
Location: Where

The line under Inside shows the place of the location it is in, such
as Office > Shelf A.
@LOC_LOC_CODE
Location: Code

Optional: a short code for labels and typing, such as B12, up to 10
characters: a letter, then letters, digits or -.  The item and search
forms take a location by it.
@LOC_CAPACITY
Location: Capacity

Optional: how much the location holds, a whole number of up to 4
digits.
@LOC_NOTES
Location: Notes

Free text about the location, on the Notes page, Ctrl+Enter starting
a new line.
@LOC_LT_NAME
Location: Type name

The name of the type in Type, shown beside it.
@CONTENTS
Contents of a location

The items at one location; the title bar shows where it is and what
is shown.

  Up, Down, PgUp, PgDn, Home, End   move through the list
  Left, Right  scroll the columns sideways, as the bar at the foot
               of a list wider than its window does
  Shift+Up, Shift+Down   select the rows passed over too
  Shift+click  select the rows up to the one clicked
  Ctrl+click   add the row clicked to the selection, or take it away
  Enter        open the selected item (a double click too)
  Ctrl+E       edit the selected item (level 2)
  Ins          a new item at this location (level 2)
  Del          delete the selected item, after asking (level 2)
  Ctrl+D       duplicate the selected item and edit the copy; Esc
               there, with nothing saved, removes the copy again
               (level 2)
  Ctrl+L, Ctrl+R, Ctrl+U   lend, reserve or mark in use the selected
               item (level 2)
  F6, Tab      beside the tree, back to the tree (Shift+F6 and
               Shift+Tab too)
  Esc          back to the locations; beside the tree, back to the
               tree
  Shift+F10    the Record menu at the selected row

The Record menu (Alt+C) moves, installs, takes out and splits the
selected item, and lists its parts, service records, loans and holds
and what is used with it, as in the item list.  Not installed only
shows only the items not installed in another, or again all of them,
installed parts included; Everything below shows the items of every
location inside this one too, at any depth, or again only its own.

The Location column names where each item is.  A location's contents
open with all its own items, and with those of every location inside
it when it holds locations, as a room holds shelves.
@BYCAT
Items by category

Items > Browse by category lists the categories, in their order, with
code, name and order number, and on the right the items of the
selected category, which follow the selection once it rests on a
category for a moment.  Drag the bar between them with the mouse to
widen either.  The Record menu, the tool bar and the count on the
status bar are those of the side that has the focus.

  Up, Down, PgUp, PgDn, Home, End   move through the list
  F6, Tab      to the items beside the categories, and back
               (Shift+F6 and Shift+Tab too); Esc there comes back
               as well
  Enter        on a category, its items as a list of their own (a
               double click too); on an item, the item
  Ctrl+E       edit the selected category's name or order number,
               or the selected item (level 2)
  Ins          a new category, or on the right a new item of the
               selected category (level 2)
  Del          delete the selected category or item, after asking
               (level 2); only a category that no item has can be
               deleted
  F5           the list with the focus again, as it is now
  Esc          back to the item list; from a category's own list
               of items, back to the categories
  Shift+F10    the Record menu at the selected row

A category is edited, added and deleted here as in Items >
Categories, in the same form and by the same rules (see Categories).
A category saved or added is selected again, wherever its order
number has put it.

The items on the right, and a category's items listed on their own,
are an item list with all its keys and commands (see Item lists):
several rows selected, Move, Install in, Take out, Split units, lend,
reserve and use, the item's parts, service records and loans, Next
order and the headings.  An item whose category is changed there, or
that is deleted, leaves the list at once; it shows under its new
category as soon as that one is selected.

View > Pane beside the list shows the categories alone, or with the
items again, and the settings file keeps that and the width of the
categories (WINPANE.CAT=).  On a serial line or a mailbox the
categories are shown alone until that entry asks for the items, as
fetching them would hold the program at each category: Enter opens
them.

See also: Categories; Item lists; The item form.
@CATS
Categories

The categories an item can have, in their order: code, name and order
number.

  Edit, Enter, Ctrl+E   change the selected category's name or
                        order number (level 2)
  New, Ins              a new category (level 2)
  Delete, Del           delete the selected category, after asking
                        (level 2); only one that no item has can be
                        deleted
  F5                    the list again, as it is now
  Close, Esc            end the list

The code is given once, when the category is made: up to four letters
and digits.  The list is sorted by the order number, 0 to 99 (a new
one without it takes 50), then by code.

Items > Browse by category lists the categories in the main window,
each with its items beside it, and edits them the same way.

See also: Items by category.
@CATS_CAT_CODE
Category: Code

The category's code, up to 4 letters and digits, given once, when the
category is made: items name their category by it.  Required.
@CATS_CAT_NAME
Category: Name

What the category is called, such as Computers: up to 20 characters.
Required.
@CATS_SORTORD
Category: Sort order

Where the category stands in the list and in the item form's list of
categories: up to 2 digits, 0 to 99, the smaller first, those of one
order by code.  A new category without it takes 50.
@LTYPES
Location types

The types a location can have, such as Room, Shelf, Box or Bin, in
their order: code, name and order number.

  Edit, Enter, Ctrl+E   change the selected type's name or order
                        number (level 2)
  New, Ins              a new type (level 2)
  Delete, Del           delete the selected type, after asking
                        (level 2); only one that no location has can
                        be deleted
  F5                    the list again, as it is now
  Close, Esc            end the list

The code is given once, when the type is made: up to eight letters and
digits.  The list is sorted by the order number, 0 to 99 (a new one
without it takes 50), then by code.
@LTYPES_LT_CODE
Location type: Code

The type's code, up to 8 letters and digits, given once, when the
type is made: locations name their type by it.  Required.
@LTYPES_LT_NAME
Location type: Name

What the type is called, such as Room or Shelf: up to 20 characters.
Required.
@LTYPES_SORTORD
Location type: Sort order

Where the type stands in the list and in the location form's list of
types: up to 2 digits, 0 to 99, the smaller first, those of one order
by code.  A new type without it takes 50.
@SERVICE
Service records

  Up, Down, PgUp, PgDn, Home, End   move through the list
  Left, Right  scroll the columns sideways, as the bar at the foot
               of a list wider than its window does
  Enter        open the selected record (a double click too)
  Ctrl+E       edit the selected record (level 2)
  Ins          a new record for the item (an item's records only,
               level 2)
  Del          delete the selected record, after asking (level 2)
  Esc          back to the item list, from an item's records to the
               list or the item form they were opened from
  Shift+F10    the Record menu at the selected row

Items > Service lists the records newest added first.  The Service
button of the item form, or Record > Service records in an item list,
lists the item's records by service date, oldest first, those without
a date before the others.
@SERVREC
The service record form

  Tab, Shift+Tab     next, previous field
  PgUp, PgDn         first, last field
  Enter              save               Esc             cancel
  Alt+Down           open the list of the types
  Ctrl+PgDn          the notes' page, and Ctrl+PgUp back
  Ctrl+Enter         a new line in the notes

A service record notes one thing done to an item: a repair, a
cleaning, a test.  Type and summary are required.
@SERVREC_MAINT_ID
Service record: Record

The record's number, up to 8 digits, which the program gives a new
record when it is saved.  It cannot be changed.
@SERVREC_ITEM_ID
Service record: Item

The ID of the item the record is about, up to 8 digits.  It cannot be
changed: a record is added from the item's own list of records.
@SERVREC_MDATE
Service record: Date

The day the work was done, typed as YYYY-MM-DD; a new record left
without a date gets today's.
@SERVREC_MTYPE
Service record: Type

What was done: RP repair, CL clean, RC recap, TS test, MD modify, IN
inspect.  Required.
@SERVREC_SUMMARY
Service record: Summary

What was done, in a line: up to 60 characters.  Required.
@SERVREC_COST
Service record: Cost

What the work cost, a number with at most two decimals, typed without
separators; empty when nothing.
@SERVREC_DONE_BY
Service record: Done by

Who did the work, a person or a shop: up to 20 characters.
@SERVREC_NOTES
Service record: Notes

Free text about the work, on the Notes page, Ctrl+Enter starting a
new line.
@LOANS
Loans and holds

  Up, Down, PgUp, PgDn, Home, End   move through the list
  Left, Right  scroll the columns sideways, as the bar at the foot
               of a list wider than its window does
  Shift+Up, Shift+Down   select the rows passed over too
  Shift+click  select the rows up to the one clicked
  Ctrl+click   add the row clicked to the selection, or take it away
  Enter        open the selected row (a double click too)
  Ctrl+E       edit the selected row (level 2)
  Ins, Ctrl+L  lend an item: type its ID, or find it with F4, then
               fill in the form; in an item's list, lend this item
               (level 2)
  Ctrl+R       reserve an item the same way (level 2)
  Ctrl+U       mark an item in use with another the same way (level 2)
  Ctrl+PgDn    the next list: open rows, open loans, reservations,
               uses, the overdue rows, all rows (ended too);
               Ctrl+PgUp the one before
  F6, Tab      beside Items > Loans and holds, to the item's history
               and back (Shift+F6 and Shift+Tab too)
  Esc          back to the item list, from an item's list to the list
               or the item form it was opened from; from the history,
               back to the rows beside it
  Shift+F10    the Record menu at the selected row

Record > Open item opens the item of the selected row, and Record >
Return or release returns a loan, or releases a reservation or a use:
type the date as YYYY-MM-DD, or leave it empty for today (level 2).

A loan lends units of an item to a borrower until a due date.  A
reservation keeps units for a person, a purpose or another item,
until a date if one is given.  In use marks units as used with
another item or somewhere, such as a monitor on a PC; the units keep
their own location.  Items > Loans and holds lists the open rows of
every kind, the earliest due first; the title bar names the list
shown.  A loan or reservation is overdue when it is still open after
its date; its row is marked in red.  The Loans button of the item
form, or Record > Loans and holds in an item list, lists every row of
the item, open and ended, in the order they were made; Used with
there lists what is reserved for the item or used with it.

Beside each list of Items > Loans and holds stands the history of the
selected row's item: every loan and hold of that item, open and
ended, following the selection once it rests on a row.  With the
focus there the keys and the Record menu are those of an item's own
list: Ins, Ctrl+L, Ctrl+R and Ctrl+U are for that item, and Return
or release ends the row selected there.  A change on either side
shows both again as they then are; when the row whose item's history
shows leaves the list, as its own return makes it leave the open
rows, the list takes the keys again.  Drag the bar between the two to
widen either; View > Pane beside the list hides the history or shows
it again, kept in the settings file (WINPANE.LOAN=).

See also: Loans, reservations and uses.
@LOAN
Loans, reservations and uses

  Tab, Shift+Tab     next, previous field
  PgUp, PgDn         first, last field
  Enter              save               Esc             cancel
  F4                 in the item field, choose the item from a list

A loan needs a borrower and a due date.  A reservation names who or
what it is for, or the item it is for, and may have a date it lasts
until.  A use names where or with what the units are used, or the
item.  An item that is disposed of or installed in an assembly cannot
be lent, reserved or used.  The date it ended is set by Return or
release in the list.
@LOAN_LOAN_ID
Loan: ID

The row's number, up to 8 digits, which the program gives a new row
when it is saved.  It cannot be changed.
@LOAN_ITEM_ID
Loan: Item

The ID of the item lent, reserved or used, up to 8 digits, asked for
before the form opens.  It cannot be changed.
@LOAN_QTY
Loan: Quantity

How many units of the item, up to 5 digits, 1 unless changed; no more
than the item has free.  It can be set only on a new row.
@LOAN_WHAT
Loan: The item

The maker and model of the item, shown beside its ID.
@LOAN_HOLDER
Loan: Borrower

Who borrows the units, or, on a reservation, Reserved for: whom or
what they are kept for, and on a use, In use with: where or with what
they are used.  Up to 30 characters.  A loan needs it; a reservation
or a use needs it or the item in the next field.
@LOAN_HOST_ID
Loan: For item

On a reservation the item the units are kept for, on a use (With
item) the item they are used with, such as the PC a monitor stands
on.  Type its ID, or text to find it, up to 30 characters: F4, or a
click on the ... button, lists the items holding it.
@LOAN_HOSTWHAT
Loan: The other item

The maker and model of the item in For item or With item, shown
beside it.
@LOAN_CONTACT
Loan: Contact

How to reach the borrower or holder, such as a telephone number: up
to 30 characters.
@LOAN_OUT_DATE
Loan: Date out

The day the units went out, a reservation's From or a use's Since,
typed as YYYY-MM-DD; a new row without it starts today.  It can be
set only on a new row.
@LOAN_DUE_DATE
Loan: Due date

The day a loan is due back, typed as YYYY-MM-DD; a loan needs it.  On
a reservation, Until: the day it lasts until, optional.  An open row
past this date is overdue and marked in red in the lists.
@LOAN_RET_DATE
Loan: Date returned

The day a loan was returned, or a reservation or use Released, set by
Return or release in the list.  It cannot be changed here.
@LOAN_NOTES
Loan: Notes

A line of your own about the row: up to 60 characters.
@SEARCH
Search

Fill in one of the fields and press Enter:

  Text         finds the text in maker, model, description, tags,
               serial number and type, in the items that match the
               filters given
  Serial       finds an exact serial number
  Category, Location, Status, Available, Tags, Works
               alone, list the items that match all the filters given

Location keeps the items at that location only: type its code, or
choose it from the list that F4 in the field, or its ... button,
shows; the line under it shows where it is, and (no location), the
list's first line, finds the items whose place is not known.  All
below also keeps the items of every location inside it.  Category,
Status and Works are chosen from their lists, each code with its name.
Available keeps the items that can be lent, reserved or used: status
A, not installed in another, with units not on loan, reserved or in
use.  It already means status A, so Status stays empty with it.  Tags
keeps the items that carry every tag given, whole (net is not network;
the Text finds parts of words); F4 in Tags, or its ... button, adds
one from the tags in use.  Works keeps the items in that state: ? not
tested, Y, I (intermittently), N, or Y?I, all but the broken ones;
empty keeps all.
The results are an item list.  Esc there comes back to this dialog
with its values kept, and Esc in the dialog then opens the item list.
@SETTINGS
Settings

These are kept in the settings file, which INV reads too.  The data
directory, the station name, the default user, the baud rate and
RTS/CTS take effect the next time the program starts; the item list
order at once.

  Tab, Shift+Tab     next, previous field
  PgUp, PgDn         first, last field
  Enter              save               Esc             cancel
  Alt+Down           open the list of a field with fixed choices
  Space              check or clear RTS/CTS

A file that cannot be written is said, with its name and what went
wrong, and the dialog comes back with the values as typed.
@SETTINGS_DIR
Settings: Data directory

The directory of the data of a local session started without one, up
to 64 characters; while none is set it shows the default, DATA under
the current directory.  It takes effect the next time the program
starts.
@SETTINGS_STA
Settings: Station name

The name this machine gives the server, up to 16 characters, which
the server's sessions and the audit show.  It takes effect the next
time the program starts.
@SETTINGS_USR
Settings: Default user

The user name the login offers, up to 8 characters.  It takes effect
the next time the program starts.
@SETTINGS_ORD
Settings: Item list order

The order the item list opens in: ID, LOC (location), MFR (maker) or
SERIAL.  It takes effect at once.
@SETTINGS_BAU
Settings: Baud rate

The speed of a serial connection to the server: 1200, 2400, 4800,
9600, 19200 or 38400, the same as the server's.  It takes effect the
next time the program starts.
@SETTINGS_RTS
Settings: RTS/CTS flow control

Checked, the serial connection uses hardware flow control, as the
server must too; Space checks or clears it.  It takes effect the next
time the program starts.
@SETTINGS_CFG
Settings: Settings file

The settings file these are kept in, INV.CFG unless the program was
started with another.  It cannot be changed here.
@SETTINGS_PRG
Settings: Program

The program and its version, to name when asking for help.
@USERS
The users

Each user logs in with a name and a password and has an access
level: 1 may only look, 2 may also change the inventory, 3 may also
manage the users and the data files.  The list shows them in name
order; Status is A for active and D for disabled, and a disabled
user's row is marked in red.  Chg is T while the user must change the
password at the next login.

  Up, Down, PgUp, PgDn, Home, End   move through the list
  Left, Right  scroll the columns sideways, as the bar at the foot
               of a list wider than its window does
  Enter        open the selected user (a double click too)
  Ctrl+E       edit the selected user
  Ins          a new user
  Del          delete the selected user, after asking
  F6, Tab      to the user's entries of the audit log, and back
               (Shift+F6 and Shift+Tab too); Esc there comes back
  Esc          back to the list it was opened from
  Shift+F10    the Record menu at the selected row

View > Pane beside the list puts beside the list the entries of the
audit log that the selected user made, the newest first: logins,
changes to the inventory and to the users.  They follow the selection
once it rests on a user, and are only to read; F1 there explains the
audit log.  On a serial line or a mailbox the entries show only once
that entry asks for them.  Drag the bar between the two to widen
either; the choice is kept in the settings file (WINPANE.USER=).

The last active user of level 3 can be neither deleted, disabled nor
given a lower level.  Only users of level 3 can open this list.

See also: The user form; Changing the password.
@USER
The user form

  Tab, Shift+Tab     next, previous field
  PgUp, PgDn         first, last field
  Enter              save               Esc             cancel
  Alt+Down           open the list of the levels or the status

A new user needs a level and a password and must change the password
at the first login.  Changes of level and status take effect at the
user's next login.
@USER_STATUS
User: Status

A active, D disabled: a disabled user can no longer log in; set it
back to A to enable the user again.  The last active user of level 3
cannot be disabled.
@USER_USERNAME
User: User name

The name the user logs in with, up to 8 letters, digits and _, given
when the user is created; it cannot be changed afterwards.
@USER_FULLNAME
User: Full name

The user's own name, which the user list shows: up to 30 characters.
@USER_GROUP
User: Group

A group of your own for the user, such as a team or a class, as
dBASE III Plus PROTECT has: up to 8 characters.  It grants nothing.
@USER_LEVEL
User: Level

What the user may do: 1 only look, 2 also change the inventory, 3
also manage the users and the data files.  Required.  The last active
user of level 3 cannot be given a lower one.
@USER_PASSWORD
User: New password

To give the user a new password, type it here and again in Repeat
password; the user must change it at the next login.  Left empty, the
password stays as it is.  A password is 4 to 16 printable characters
without spaces, and the field takes no more; one too short is refused.
It is not shown while you type.
@USER_REPEAT
User: Repeat password

The new password again, which must be the same as the one typed
above.  It is not shown while you type.
@ADMIN
Backup, restore, pack and reindex

These work on the data files as a whole and need the system to
themselves: other users must log out first.  Each can take minutes
when there is much data.  A directory named here is one on the machine
that holds the data: the server, or this machine when working locally.

  Backup    copies every table, notes file and index into a directory
            that exists already; a backup there before is replaced
  Restore   replaces all the data with the backup in a directory,
            after asking; the indexes are rebuilt from its tables
  Pack      removes the deleted records for good, after asking, and
            rebuilds every index; deleted items can then no longer be
            brought back
  Reindex   rebuilds every index from its table, as after a damaged
            index

Each is written to the audit log.  They need access level 3.
@AUDIT
The audit log

Every change to the data is written to the audit log: who made it
(user and station), when, the table, the record, the field, and its
old and new value.  Logins, failed logins, password changes and the
work on the data files are there too.  The list shows the newest rows
first; with a table it shows that table's rows by record, oldest
first.  Failed logins are marked in red.

  Table        ITEMS, LOCS, CATS, LTYPES, USERS, MAINT, LOANS, or
               AUDIT for the work on the data files
  Record       the record's ID (for CATS and LTYPES its number in the
               file); it needs a table
  Station      the station the changes came from

Enter shows the list with these filters; empty ones filter nothing.
In the list Esc goes back to the list the audit log was opened from;
Left and Right, as the bar at its foot, scroll its columns sideways.
Values longer than their column are cut.  Packing renumbers the
records of CATS and LTYPES; rows written before it keep the old
numbers.  The audit log needs access level 3.

Beside Admin > Users the same list shows what the selected user did,
with no filter of its own.
@SESSIONS
The sessions

The sessions open on the server, yours selected: the station, the
user, the access level, the state, and the seconds since the
session's last request.  A station that has not logged in shows no
user and level 0.  Refresh, or F5 in the list, asks the server again;
Close or Esc ends the dialog.

Only a server has sessions: working locally, the dialog says this is
not available.  It needs access level 3.
@EXPORT
Export to a CSV file

Writes every record of a table to a text file of comma-separated
values, one line per record, the way dBASE III Plus writes with
COPY TO file DELIMITED.  Notes are not written.

  Table        ITEMS, LOCS, CATS, LTYPES (the location types), MAINT,
               LOANS or AUDIT
  File         the file to write, on the machine that holds the data:
               the server, or this machine when working locally
  Field names  checked, the field names are the first line

  Tab, Shift+Tab     next, previous field
  PgUp, PgDn         first, last field
  Enter              export             Esc             cancel
  Alt+Down           open the list of the tables

Working locally, a file name dialog comes first, listing this
machine's directories and their CSV files; over a server the file is
typed as the server names it.  While the export runs, the status bar
counts the records written, and Esc stops it; the file is then left
as it was, since it gets its name only once complete.  What was
written so far stays on the machine that holds the data, under the
file's name with an extension starting &C.  A file that exists
already is replaced only after you agree.  Export and import need
access level 3.
@IMPORT
Import a CSV file

Adds the lines of a text file of comma-separated values to ITEMS or
LOCS as new records, checked as a new item or location typed in a
form would be.  Nothing that exists is changed or deleted.

  Table        ITEMS or LOCS
  File         the file to read, on the machine that holds the data
  Field names  checked when the first line names the columns;
               otherwise the columns are the table's fields in their
               order, without the notes

ITEMS: ITEM_ID CATEGORY ITEMTYPE MFR MODEL SERIALNO DESCR COND QTY
ON_LOAN RESERVED IN_USE ATTACHED LOC_ID PARENT_ID ACQ_DATE ACQ_SRC
ACQ_PRICE CUR_VALUE TAGS STATUS CREATED UPDATED.  LOCS: LOC_ID
PARENT_ID LOC_NAME LOC_CODE LTYPE CAPACITY.  Dates are YYYYMMDD;
CATEGORY, COND and STATUS take the codes of the item form, LTYPE a
location type's code.

Working locally, a file name dialog comes first, as for an export.
A value in quotes is kept as it is, with "" standing for one quote.
An empty value takes the default of a new record.  The columns the
program fills in itself (item ID, location ID, on loan, reserved, in
use, attached, the assembly, the dates created and updated) are
ignored, so an exported file can be imported back: the records get
new IDs.  An item's LOC_ID takes a
location's number or its code.  A location's PARENT_ID takes a code,
naming a location already kept or one on any line of the file, or a
number: when the file has a LOC_ID column (every export, and every
file without field names) the number names the line of the file with
that LOC_ID, otherwise a location already kept.  A location may come
before or after the ones inside it.

Every line is checked before anything is added.  If any line is
refused, nothing is added, and the refused lines are listed with
their reasons.  A disk error while the records are being added can
stop the import with some of them added; the message says how many.
The import needs the system to itself: other users must log out
first.  A large file takes minutes on a slow machine.  Export and
import need access level 3.
@REPORTS
Reports

Fill in the options and press Enter; the dialog opens again for
another run until Esc.

  Sort by      item listing: by location (grouped, with the items and
               the quantity of each location), by ID, or by maker
  Category, Location
               only the items of that category, chosen from its list,
               or location, chosen with F4 or the ... button; the
               line under Location shows where it is
  All below    checked: also the items of every location inside the
               one given; the report then goes by location
  Status       item listing: ALL, A (active) or D (disposed of)
  Works, Tags  listing, valuation and item labels: only the items in
               that works state and with every tag given, as in Search
               (Works ? lists the untested stock)
  Overdue, As of
               loans: only those due before the date; the date marks
               the late loans with ! and heads the pages (empty:
               today)
  Output       SCREEN (shown in the preview), the first offered,
               PRINTER (Windows' default printer) or FILE (the text a
               plain printer would get, into the file named; an
               existing file is replaced only after asking)

By location, each location has a heading with its place, such as
Office > Shelf A, in the order of the location list; the items whose
place is not known come first.  The valuation lists the active items
by location with the price paid and the value, subtotals per location,
the total, and totals by category.  The amounts are each item's own,
added as stored: they are not multiplied by the quantity.

While a report runs, a dialog counts the pages and the records; its
Cancel, or Esc, stops it: the report then ends with a line saying so,
and prints no totals.  The page length is set in INV's settings, kept
in the same settings file.

In the preview the arrows, PgUp, PgDn, Home and End move through the
report, and Esc, Enter or Close on its system menu end it.

See also: Labels.
@LABELS
Labels

Item labels: one label per item, with its ID and category, maker and
model, serial number and location.  Type an item ID for one label;
otherwise the labels of all items, or of the category, location, works
state and tags given (with All below, also of the locations inside
it), in the order of the location list.  Category is chosen from its
list; F4 in Location or Tags, or the ... button beside it, lists them
to choose from.

Bin labels: one label per location, with its name in double width
where the printer has such a font, else in bold, its code if it has
one, its place (such as Office > Shelf A > Bin 12) and its type.  Give
a location for its label, with All below checked also for every
location inside it; leave it empty for all locations.

  Output       SCREEN, PRINTER or FILE, as for reports

Labels are printed on continuous stock, one label across.  The label
width and the pitch (lines from one label to the next) are set in
INV's settings, kept in the same settings file.  Line the first label
up by hand before printing.
@KEYS
Keys everywhere

  F1           help on the list or dialog in use
  F10          the menu bar
  Alt+letter   the menu of that letter (Alt alone, then the arrows);
               in a dialog, the field or button whose letter is
               underlined
  Ctrl+F       the Find box of the tool bar: type a text, then
               Enter; Esc or Tab goes back to the list
  F5           the list shown again, as it is now, or the preview
               beside it while that has the focus (View > Refresh)
  F6           beside a list with a pane, the other side
  Shift+F10    the Record menu, at the selected row of the list
  Alt+F4       exit
  Esc          back, cancel, close
  Enter        open, or the dialog's OK
  Tab          in a dialog, the next field (Shift+Tab the previous)

The Record menu (Alt+C) holds the commands of the list shown (beside
the location tree, the side with the focus), each beside its key when
it has one; choosing one is pressing the key.  Commands used less
often, such as Move or Parts, are in the menu alone.  A click with the
right mouse button on a row of the list selects it and opens the
Record menu there.  File > Log out and File > Exit end the login and
the program.  An entry ending in ... opens a dialog first.  While the
item list shows, the Items menu checks the Browse entry of its order.
View > Pane beside the list shows or hides what stands beside the
item lists, a preview of the selected item, beside the assemblies,
the selected one's parts, beside the loans and holds, the history of
the selected row's item, beside the users, the selected user's
entries of the audit log, and beside the location tree, its items;
it is grey on the other lists.  On a serial line or a mailbox each
list shows alone until this entry asks for what stands beside it.
Help > Help on this screen is F1 for the list shown, and Help >
Keyboard shows these keys.  Menu entries drawn grey need a higher
access level than yours, or, in the Record menu, a selected row or
another list.

Help opens in Windows Help (WINHELP.EXE), where Index lists the
topics, Back returns to the one before and Search finds a topic by its
title.

Without Windows Help, or without INVWIN.HLP beside the program, help
opens in a window of its own: it scrolls with the arrows, PgUp, PgDn,
Ctrl+Home and Ctrl+End, F1 in it shows these keys, and Esc or Enter
closes it.

The tool bar under the menus gives New, Open, Edit, Delete, Move,
Install and Lend of the Record menu, each grey where its entry is,
and the Find box: Enter or the Find button beside it lists the items
holding its text; empty, it opens Search > Find text.

The status bar at the bottom names the connection, your user name and
your level, then, on a local, NetBIOS or TCP/IP connection, the count
of the list shown.  It is counted as the list opens and when its order
changes, and follows the records you add, duplicate, split off and
delete.  Its last panel describes the menu entry highlighted, and says
what a command changed until the next key or click.

The headings above a list name its columns.  Drag the line at the
right of a heading with the mouse to widen or narrow that column; Esc
cancels the drag.  The width holds for every list of those columns
and is kept in the settings file (WINCOLS.).
